@startuml deployment-diagram
title Route Optimization Platform — Deployment Diagram (Deployment View)

node "Client Machine" as ClientNode {
  [API Client] as Client
}

node "Docker Host (VPS)" as Host {
  node "Docker Container\n(python:3.12-slim)" as Container {
    component "Flask API Process\n(Web/app.py, port 5022)" as API
    component "Solver Pipeline\n(CP-SAT/main.py + CP-SAT/vehicle_routes.py\n+ CP-SAT/loader_routes.py)" as Pipeline
    folder "/ROP/data/\n(input.json, output.json —\nlive scratch files)" as DataDir
    folder "/ROP/data/inputs/, /ROP/data/outputs/\n(per-calculation snapshots,\n{calculation_id}.json)" as Snapshots
    database "/ROP/data/history.db\n(SQLite, calculation_history table)" as HistoryDB
  }
}

Client -down-> API : HTTP\n(port 5022, X-API-Key header)
API -right-> Pipeline : in-process call\n(background thread)
Pipeline -down-> DataDir : read/write JSON
API -down-> DataDir : read/write JSON
API -down-> Snapshots : write input/output\nper calculation_id
API -down-> HistoryDB : SQL read/write\n(calculation_history)

note bottom of DataDir
  data/ is COPY'd into the image at build time
  (Dockerfile: COPY data/ data/), and WORKDIR
  is /ROP, so the process resolves DATA_DIR to
  /ROP/data.
  docker-compose.yml mounts ./data:/MVPv2.2/data
  as a volume — a DIFFERENT container path that
  the Flask process never reads from or writes
  to. As a result, input.json/output.json,
  history.db, and the per-calculation snapshots
  currently do NOT persist across container
  rebuilds. This mismatch pre-dates this update
  and is worth fixing independently (align the
  volume path with /ROP/data, or vice versa).
end note

note right of HistoryDB
  New in this version (ADR-007). Single embedded
  SQLite file — no separate database container.
  Single-writer: a second API replica sharing
  this file would risk lock contention, matching
  the existing single-instance constraint on
  solver_state (ADR-002).
end note

note right of Container
  Single container, single process.
  No reverse proxy, load balancer,
  or orchestration layer defined.
end note

@enduml
